## Linear

- For work likely to span sessions or require coordination, ask whether to track it unless a relevant Linear issue already exists, and include the issue identifier in branch, commit, and PR names when relevant.
- Before Linear read/write, confirm that the repository owner matches the Linear workspace: `tifandotme` → `Citra`; `hadl-labs` → `Hadl`. If the remote is missing or has another owner, ask the user.
- In the Hadl workspace, do not move issues to Done unless the user explicitly tells you to.

## Hadl production

- Applies to manual changes to production systems or data operated by Hadl (such as a deploy, restart, migration, config or secret change, or data write). Only a change that reaches production automatically when an approved PR merges needs no announcement; the PR approval is the ack.
- A production deploy that someone triggers (a `workflow_dispatch` run, a tag pick, a rollout or restart workflow, a `kamal` command) is a manual change, even when it ships an approved PR through CI/CD.
- Before a manual change, draft an announcement for Slack #engineering (`C0AEV5BP35W`) in the user's voice and show it to the user. Post it only after the user confirms.
- After posting, wait until any engineer replies in the thread, then do the action. Do not require a specific person. If nobody replies, ask the user instead of proceeding.
- A database migration is the exception: after posting, apply it without waiting for a reply.
- This does not apply to personal tools or infrastructure just because they work with `hadl-labs` repositories.

## Completion

- Keep going when the next step does not need the user's input. Ask before destructive or hard-to-undo actions, or when you cannot continue without a decision.
- End task responses with one compact `Next:` line. List required actions before optional follow-up; write `Next: none` when complete.
- State `Status: Blocked` only when blocked; omit status otherwise. Claim completion only after verifying every explicit requirement; cite evidence and give exact commands or paths for user actions.

## Repository context

- Before working in a project area, find and read applicable `GLOSSARY.md` files. Use their ubiquitous language consistently.

## Writing and documentation

- Apply ASD-STE100 to documentation when appropriate.
- Docs: when editing markdown for agents or contributors, keep runnable shell commands and concrete repo paths first; omit redundant meta commentary.
- Markdown: format Markdown files with `oxfmt --write <path-to-file.md>`.
- Format prose with Markdown, not ANSI terminal escape sequences. Before saving prose through MCP tools, verify it contains no unintended control characters.
- Human-facing prose: for new files meant for humans to read, load `writing-clearly-and-concisely` first and `humanizer` last; do not use em dashes.
- Replies to review comments or other claims: before a draft agrees with a claim, verify it against the code, docs, source, or data, and tell the user how you checked. If you could not verify it, say so in the draft instead of agreeing.

## Git and GitHub

- Git commits: use Conventional Commits only (`fix`, `feat`, `build`, `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `revert`). Keep headers under 72 chars, lowercase subject, no period, prefer header-only.

## Browser, web, and cloud tools

- X/Twitter: use `bird` instead of browser automation.
- Browser use: load `agent-browser` first and use a task-scoped session as its skill directs. On macOS, connect that session to Helium for Agents with `agent-browser connect 9222`; if Helium is unavailable or the connection launches a fresh profile, close the session, run `run-helium`, and reconnect. On headless Linux, use `agent-browser`'s managed headless browser directly and diagnose launch failures with `agent-browser doctor`. For Electron, follow the project `AGENTS.md` and its project-specific CDP port.
- Authenticated browsing: load `authenticating-browsers` before using saved website logins, refreshing login seeds on macOS, or diagnosing Chrome's sandbox on the headless Linux VPS (hostname `box`). Follow the documented per-site workflow; keep login seeds and runtime credentials out of logs and review artifacts.
- Google Cloud: use mise-provided `CLOUDSDK_ACTIVE_CONFIG_NAME`; never run `gcloud config configurations activate`. Account mappings: `personal` → `ifan.anvity@gmail.com`, `work` → `tifan@hadl.ai`. If credentials are missing, run `gcloud auth login <account> --no-activate`; never omit `--no-activate`. If work gcloud reports `Reauthentication failed`, run `mise run gcloud-auth-check` from `~/projects/work`; its reauth task uses the configured OS keyring. Report unavailable keyring access as a blocker. Never ask for, read, or type the password yourself.
- Library docs: use Context7 for current docs. If it has no relevant results, check official docs or source; say it lacked coverage and name the source used.

## Remote and headless work

- Before desktop-dependent tasks, identify the execution host and available capabilities. `box` is headless Linux; the viewing device may be elsewhere, even when executing on macOS.
- On `box`, `$HOME/projects` is served directly at `https://box.javanese-pound.ts.net:8443/`, tailnet-only. Return links with URL-encoded paths relative to `$HOME/projects`; files and relative assets stay in their project, with no publishing copy. Links show live edits and stop working when files or worktrees are deleted. For artifacts outside this tree, use an attachment or ask before adding a route. Chezmoi manages this persistent endpoint. Diagnose failures without asking the user to start it. Viewers need Tailscale.
- For other remote handoffs, deliver reachable URLs or attachments, not executor-local paths or `localhost`. If unavailable, give exact SSH forwarding or file-transfer commands for the viewing device.
- Check `tailscale serve status` before using Serve; preserve unrelated routes. Keep port 8443 tailnet-only and dev servers on loopback. Ask before adding routes. For explicit requests to share files publicly, check or stop a public share, load `sharing-public-files`. Public sharing uses separate port 443 and selected files only; never expose the project tree, secrets, or debugging ports through Funnel.
- For watchers, dev servers, and background shells, use task-owned Herdr panes when inside Herdr (load its skill); otherwise use harness-supported long-running processes. Match the pane's shell. Report temporary previews' URLs, access requirements, lifetime, and stop commands.
- Verify artifact URLs, assets, and interactions using the browser workflow above. Distinguish server checks from confirmed device access; request a device check only when troubleshooting access.
- Use documented device-code or remote OAuth flows; loopback callbacks target the execution host. Keep credentials out of artifacts and URLs. Replace desktop-only `open` commands, clipboard writes, and notifications with conversation content or links. Finish headless checks, then hand off native-app, simulator, desktop-session, or hardware checks to a capable machine.

## Shell and code quality

- Shell scripts: after modifying one, run `shfmt -w -ln <bash|posix|mksh|bats|zsh> <path-to-script>` with its dialect. Run `shellcheck <path-to-script>` for Bash or POSIX-compatible scripts, `bash -n <path-to-script>` for Bash, and `zsh -n <path-to-script>` for Zsh. Prefer `#!/usr/bin/env <interpreter>` over hardcoded paths like `#!/bin/bash`.
- Code cleanup: after a fix changes direction, audit for speculative or now-dead code from earlier attempts. Remove unused helpers/imports/types before final checks, then rerun the relevant typecheck/lint/format commands.
- Code comments: never leave tombstone comments (e.g. `# foo removed — reason`) or incident history: what an earlier version did, what broke, how it was found, who found it, ticket and PR numbers for the fix. Say what the code does and why it is this way now. Git blame and the commit message carry the story for anyone who wants it.
- UI: Keep copy task-focused. Use clear guidance for empty states and errors; avoid decorative narrative.

## Worktrees

- When running in Herdr, prefer `herdr worktree` for a separate write-owning task that needs its own checkout. Let native subagents share their parent checkout. Remove a worktree with the tool that created it.
- When a task wraps up (its PR is merged, or the user closes it), clean up what you created for it: stop each agent session you started there, run `herdr worktree remove --workspace <id>` without `--force`, and delete the merged local branch. When the worktree is dirty or holds commits the merged PR lacks, stop and report instead.
- Put worktrees directly under their project group: `~/projects/{work|personal}/<repo-name>-<branch-name>`.
- Replace `/` in branch names with `-` when forming the directory name. If the destination exists, stop and report the collision instead of choosing another path.
- Pass the absolute destination with `--path` and the chosen branch with `--branch` to `herdr worktree create`. Herdr does not choose this path for the local workflow.
- Keep session data in each harness's supported storage and resume mechanism. Do not move or edit session files to imitate migration.
- The project picker removes only clean worktrees merged into the default branch, and refuses worktrees containing ignored files.
- `prune-worktrees` repairs leftovers of a folder deleted outside Herdr: stale Git registrations and Herdr workspaces whose folder is gone. It is for repairs only, never part of task cleanup. Agents preview with `prune-worktrees [repo] </dev/null`; the user confirms in a terminal.
- Keep one writing agent per worktree at a time. To move a task to a different agent, stop the first session, then start the next session in the same worktree.
- When a task spans more than one checkout, put the target in every git and gh command (`git -C <path>`, `gh --repo <owner/name>`); the shell's working directory can reset between calls.
